From cddd9d9b65267361b5f09cac7cb0e069623a65d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 06:48:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Route the player settings - the last family that never
 crossed the wire

Turn 4670 this time, and the logs name it exactly:

  inventory 1 (owner=1)  host: generic=27  r24=15 r25=15
                         peer: generic=12  no swords, no shields

Fifteen generic serfs became knights on one machine, consuming fifteen
swords and fifteen shields, and stayed generic on the other. That is the
knights menu, and every settings popup was doing the same thing: calling
straight into the local player.

There were about fifty such call sites - food and plank and steel and coal
and wheat distribution, tool priorities, knight occupation, castle
garrison, serf-to-knight rate, the priority resets, cycle knights, promote
to knights, and attacking. All of them change how the simulation behaves,
so all of them are commands.

They travel as one NetCmd.player_setting with a NetSetting id rather than
thirty command kinds, because the shape is identical: a setting, a value,
and occasionally a small index. Every call site is now one line through
net_player_setting(), which either queues it or applies it, and
net_apply_setting() is the single place a setting is applied - so the
local path and the networked path cannot drift into meaning different
things.

Two needed more than a substitution. Promoting knights returns a count the
popup used to pick its sound; networked it cannot know, so it clicks and
lets the simulation answer. Attacking sends the target building and the
number of knights, and the far side runs knights_available_for_attack()
itself to work out which of its own buildings can reach the target - the
list is derived, so it must not be trusted from the wire. That also puts
those fields back in step: the attack box fills them in locally so it has
something to draw.

send_strongest() turned out to be a getter, not an action, so it stays
where it is.

And the players hash now covers the settings themselves, not just the
three score totals. Nothing was watching them, which is why a knights-menu
click surfaced thirty turns later as serfs in the wrong state - a
simulation bug is what that looks like, and it was not one.

